# HTML — Markup Standards


## Baseline


- Target the **WHATWG HTML Living Standard**; accessibility target is **WCAG 2.2 level AA**.
- Markup describes **meaning**, CSS describes **presentation**, JS adds **behaviour** —
  the page must stay usable (content readable, links working) before JS loads.
- Indent with 2 spaces, lowercase element/attribute names, always quote attribute values.

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>Orders — Acme Shop</title>
  <meta name="description" content="Overview of your recent orders.">
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/app.css">
  <script type="module" src="/assets/app.js"></script>
</head>
```

- `lang` on `<html>` is mandatory (screen readers, hyphenation, translation); mark
  inline language changes with `lang` on the element.
- Unique, descriptive `<title>` per page: specific part first, site name last.

## Semantic Structure


- Use landmarks: `<header>`, `<nav>`, `<main>` (exactly one), `<aside>`, `<footer>`;
  `<article>` / `<section>` only with a heading.
- **One `<h1>` per page**; heading levels never skip (`h2` → `h4` is a bug). Choose the
  level by document outline, not by font size.
- Native element first — ARIA only when no native element exists ("no ARIA is better than bad ARIA").

```html
<!-- ❌ Not focusable, no keyboard support, no role -->
<div class="btn" onclick="save()">Save</div>

<!-- ✅ Native button: focus, Enter/Space, role — all for free -->
<button type="button" id="save">Save</button>

<!-- ✅ Navigation = link; action = button -->
<a href="/orders/42">Order #42</a>
```

- Prefer native interactive elements over custom widgets: `<dialog>` (modal, focus trap,
  Esc), `popover` attribute, `<details>`/`<summary>`, `<input type="date">`.
- Lists are `<ul>`/`<ol>`, tabular data is `<table>` with `<caption>` and `<th scope>` —
  never tables for layout.

## Accessibility


- Every `<img>` has `alt`: a description of its **purpose** for content images, `alt=""`
  for decorative ones.
- Every interactive element has an **accessible name** — visible text, `<label>`, or
  `aria-label` for icon-only controls.
- Everything works with the **keyboard only**; focus order follows DOM order; never
  remove the focus outline without a `:focus-visible` replacement; no positive `tabindex`.
- Text contrast ≥ 4.5:1 (large text ≥ 3:1); information is never conveyed by color alone.
- Link text makes sense out of context — ❌ "click here", ✅ "Download the 2026 report (PDF, 2 MB)".
- Dynamic status messages use `role="status"` / `aria-live="polite"`; errors use `role="alert"`.

## Forms


```html
<form action="/account/email" method="post" novalidate>
  <label for="email">E-mail</label>
  <input id="email" name="email" type="email" autocomplete="email" required
         aria-describedby="email-hint email-error">
  <p id="email-hint">We never share your e-mail.</p>
  <p id="email-error" role="alert" hidden></p>

  <input type="hidden" name="csrf_token" value="{{ csrf_token }}">
  <button type="submit">Save</button>
</form>
```

- Every input has a programmatically associated `<label>` — a `placeholder` is not a label.
- Use the correct `type`, `autocomplete`, and `inputmode` — mobile keyboards and password
  managers depend on them.
- Group related controls with `<fieldset>` + `<legend>` (radio groups, addresses).
- Client-side validation is UX only — the **server always validates again**.
- State-changing requests use `method="post"` with a CSRF token; never GET.

## Performance (Core Web Vitals)


```html
<!-- ✅ LCP image: eager, high priority, explicit size (prevents layout shift / CLS) -->
<img src="/img/hero.avif" alt="Team at work" width="1200" height="600" fetchpriority="high">

<!-- ✅ Below-the-fold: lazy + responsive -->
<img src="/img/p-800.webp" alt="Product photo" width="800" height="600" loading="lazy"
     decoding="async"
     srcset="/img/p-400.webp 400w, /img/p-800.webp 800w" sizes="(max-width: 600px) 100vw, 800px">
```

- Always set `width`/`height` (or CSS `aspect-ratio`) on images, video, and iframes — prevents CLS.
- Never `loading="lazy"` on the LCP image; mark it `fetchpriority="high"`.
- Scripts: `type="module"` or `defer` — never a blocking `<script>` in `<head>`.
- Modern formats (AVIF/WebP) via `<picture>` or `srcset`; `preconnect` only for critical third-party origins.

## Security


- **No inline JavaScript**: no `onclick=""`, no `javascript:` URLs, no inline `<script>`
  blocks without a CSP nonce — this is what makes a strict Content-Security-Policy possible.
- Serve a strict CSP (nonce- or hash-based, `object-src 'none'`, `base-uri 'none'`) as an
  HTTP header, not a `<meta>` tag.
- Third-party assets from a CDN carry `integrity` (SRI) + `crossorigin="anonymous"`.
  Prefer self-hosting.
- External links with `target="_blank"` add `rel="noopener noreferrer"`.
- Server-side templates: rely on the engine's **auto-escaping** (Twig, Blade `{{ }}`,
  Jinja2 autoescape); raw output (`|raw`, `{!! !!}`, `|safe`) only for sanitized HTML,
  with a comment stating where it was sanitized.

## Styling Hooks


- No `style=""` attributes and no presentational elements (`<b>`, `<font>`, `<center>`) —
  use classes; `<strong>`/`<em>` only for semantic emphasis.
- Do not style by `id`; `id` is for fragment links, `<label for>`, and ARIA references.
- Test hooks use `data-testid` (or role/label queries) — never CSS classes.

## Verification


- Markup is validated (`html-validate`) and accessibility-checked (axe-core) before a
  Task is done; automated checks catch ~40 % of WCAG issues — also do a keyboard-only pass.
  Commands: `skills/node-dev/SKILL.md`.
